# Effective Harness for Long Running Agents
Reference Papers:
[Effective Harness for Long Running Agents](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-harnesses-for-long-running-agents)
[Agentic Memory](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.01885)

## Project Description



## specifications

decomposition of agents

### manager:
owns the task state 

### worker:
idempotent, stateless
picks up task runs until completion
doesnt hold context
    - tool sidecar
    Safety Interception: Inspects commands against safety policies to block destructive commands (rm -rf /, mkfs, etc.).
    Output Truncation: Automatically compresses large compiler / test outputs (preserving 40 head lines and 40 tail lines   with a truncation notice) to prevent context window exhaustion.
    Operations: Provides secure, sandboxed handlers for BASH, FILE_WRITE, FILE_READ, RUN_TESTS, and GIT_DIFF.

    - worker pod

    MicroPlanner: Formulates 2–4 immediate sub-actions based on the task description and injected memory briefing.
    ActionEngine: Drives step-by-step execution through the ToolSidecar.
    WorkspaceTracker: Captures git diffs and records task-scoped atomic commit hashes.
    Worker: Coordinates the micro-loop, halting on failures and returning a structured ExecutionResult

### evaluator:
audits worker
if task pass the audit -> manager marks task as done
if task fail the audit -> feedback to worker, worker retry

### memory and knowledge
├── Episodic
│
├── Semantic
│
├── Procedural
│
└── Epistemic

## Harness
harness owns reliability and reproducibility 

- The Loop Controller (orchestrates manager -> worker -> evaluator)
- Memory System (stores episodic / semantic / procedural / epistemic knowledge)
- Reliable Messaging (ensure task status propagates even if components crash)

## Data flow
1. A high-level task is received by the Loop Controller.
2. The Loop Controller creates a new TaskContext in the database.
3. The Loop Controller updates the task status to RUNNING and triggers the Manager agent.
4. The Manager agent retrieves the TaskContext, plans an initial sub-task, and sets it as the current_sub_task.
5. The Loop Controller detects the new sub-task and assigns it to an available Worker.
6. The Worker retrieves the sub-task, retrieves the TaskContext for briefing, and interacts with the ToolSidecar to execute atomic steps.
7. After each atomic step, the Worker updates the ToolLog, updates the sub-task status (e.g., COMPLETED), and then calls UpdateSubTask.
8. The Manager agent receives the UpdateSubTask event and, upon completion of all sub-tasks, triggers the Evaluator agent.
9. The Evaluator agent inspects the results and either marks the task as DONE or sends feedback to the Manager for retry.
10. Reliable Messaging ensures that if any component crashes (e.g., the Manager), the Loop Controller detects the failure and restarts the component from a consistent state.

